---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const writable = `let cfg = ConfigStack::new()
    .layer("defaults", defaults)
    .layer("project", project)
    .write_to("project")
    .build()?;

cfg.set_val("server.port", 8080u16)?;`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Reading and writing with ConfigStack — dotcfg"
  description="Read resolved ConfigStack values and choose exactly which source receives writes."
>
  <article class="[&_p]:max-w-[720px]">
    <span class="text-[13px] font-bold text-primary">ConfigStack</span>
    <h1 class="m-0 mb-5 mt-3 text-[clamp(38px,7vw,64px)] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.045em] max-[760px]:text-[44px]">
      Reading & writing
    </h1>
    <p class="max-w-[680px] text-xl text-muted-foreground max-[760px]:text-lg">
      Reading from several sources has a natural answer: return the value that wins after the layers
      are combined. Writing has an extra question: which file should dotcfg change?
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Read the final configuration
    </h2>
    <p>
      ConfigStack gives you familiar read methods. <code>get()</code> reads one value as a string,
      <code>get_as()</code> reads one value as a Rust type, and <code>load()</code> deserializes the
      complete resolved configuration into your struct.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Rust"
      code={'let port: u16 = cfg.get_as("server.port")?;\nlet config: Option<AppConfig> = cfg.load()?;'}
    />
    <p>
      These methods read the <em>result</em> of the stack, not one particular file. That means a
      value may come from an earlier layer, a later layer, or the environment depending on what is
      configured.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Why writes need a target
    </h2>
    <p>
      Suppose <code>server.port</code> exists in both <code>defaults</code> and{' '}
      <code>project</code>. If you call <code>set_val("server.port", 8080)</code>, ConfigStack
      cannot safely guess which file you intended to edit.
    </p>
    <p>
      You choose the writable source with <code>write_to()</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Rust" code={writable} />
    <p>
      In this example, stack-level writes go only to <code>project</code>. The defaults source is
      left untouched.
    </p>
    <Callout type="info" title="No write_to() means read-only">
      <p>
        A stack without a write target can still be read normally. If you try to call{' '}
        <code>set()</code>, <code>set_val()</code>, or <code>save()</code>, dotcfg returns{' '}
        <code>Error::NoWriteTarget</code> instead of choosing a file for you.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      save() does not copy the whole stack
    </h2>
    <p>
      <code>save()</code> writes the struct you give it to the selected write target. It does not
      take values from the other layers or from environment variables and copy them into that file.
    </p>
    <p>
      This matters because the layers still own their own data. A system defaults file should not
      suddenly be copied into a project file just because you saved project settings.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Work with one source directly
    </h2>
    <p>
      Sometimes you do not want the final combined value. You may want to inspect the defaults
      source itself, or intentionally change one particular source. Use <code>source()</code> for
      that.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Rust"
      code={'let defaults = cfg.source("defaults")?;\nlet port: u16 = defaults.get_as("server.port")?;'}
    />
    <p>
      <code>source()</code> returns that source's <code>DotCfg</code>, so its normal DotCfg
      behavior applies. The other layers and the stack's environment prefix are not part of this
      direct read.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Errors that protect the stack
    </h2>
    <p>
      Layer names must be unique. <code>build()</code> returns <code>Error::DuplicateSource</code>
      if the same name is added twice. If <code>write_to()</code> names a source that was never
      added, it returns <code>Error::UnknownSource</code>. These checks happen when you build the
      stack so configuration mistakes are caught before the stack is used.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
